\section{Numerical Methods}
\label{sec:numerical_methods}

\subsection{Overview}
The AAAHSC--9.4 architecture implements a fully coupled relativistic
simulation framework based on the BSSN $3+1$ formulation of the Einstein
field equations, integrated over a block-structured adaptive mesh
refinement (AMR) hierarchy. The system is designed for secure ingestion
of multi-messenger astrophysical telemetry, GPU-resident refinement
operations, and continuous constraint monitoring to ensure numerical
homeostasis across multiple physical regimes.

\subsection{Spacetime Foliation and Evolution Equations}
We adopt the standard BSSN decomposition of the spacetime metric,
splitting the $4$-dimensional manifold into spatial hypersurfaces
$\Sigma_t$ with induced metric $\gamma_{ij}$ and extrinsic curvature
$K_{ij}$. The evolved variables include:
\begin{equation}
    \tilde{\gamma}_{ij}, \quad
    \phi, \quad
    \tilde{A}_{ij}, \quad
    K, \quad
    \tilde{\Gamma}^i,
\end{equation}
where tildes denote conformally rescaled quantities. Gauge evolution is
performed using a $1+\log$ lapse and Gamma-driver shift condition:
\begin{align}
    \partial_t \alpha &= -2 \alpha K, \\
    \partial_t \beta^i &= \frac{3}{4} B^i, \\
    \partial_t B^i &= \partial_t \tilde{\Gamma}^i - \eta B^i.
\end{align}

Spatial derivatives are discretized using fourth-order finite differences,
and time integration is performed using a fourth-order Runge--Kutta
scheme. All evolution equations are evaluated independently on each AMR
block, with inter-block synchronization handled by GPU-resident kernels.

\subsection{Adaptive Mesh Refinement (AMR)}
The AMR hierarchy consists of $L = 7$ refinement levels, each with
uniform Cartesian grids. Refinement is triggered by curvature-based
criteria:
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{C}(x) = \left| R(x) \right| + \left| K(x) \right|,
\end{equation}
and by apparent horizon detection using the null-expansion scalar
$\theta$:
\begin{equation}
    \theta \le 10^{-3} \quad \Rightarrow \quad \text{refine}.
\end{equation}

Each refinement step performs:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item prolongation of coarse-grid data to finer levels,
    \item restriction of fine-grid data to coarse levels,
    \item flux correction across refinement boundaries,
    \item ghost-zone population for stencil completeness.
\end{enumerate}

All operations are executed via elementwise CUDA kernels, eliminating
CPU-side AMR control and minimizing PCIe transfer overhead.

\subsection{GPU-Resident Boundary Kernels}
The boundary-update subsystem is implemented entirely on NVIDIA GPUs.
Each AMR block is mapped to a CUDA thread block, and refinement
operations are performed using warp-synchronous primitives. The kernels
implement:
\begin{align}
    \text{Prolongation:} &\quad Q^{\ell+1} = \mathcal{P}(Q^\ell), \\
    \text{Restriction:}  &\quad Q^\ell = \mathcal{R}(Q^{\ell+1}), \\
    \text{Flux Sync:}    &\quad F^\ell = \mathcal{S}(F^{\ell+1}),
\end{align}
where $\ell$ indexes refinement levels. Memory access patterns are
optimized for coalesced reads and writes, ensuring minimal latency during
boundary updates.

\subsection{Constraint Monitoring and Numerical Homeostasis}
The ADM Hamiltonian and momentum constraints are computed at each
timestep:
\begin{align}
    \mathcal{H} &= R + K^2 - K_{ij} K^{ij}, \\
    \mathcal{M}^i &= D_j (K^{ij} - \gamma^{ij} K).
\end{align}

Global norms are evaluated using:
\begin{equation}
    \| \mathcal{H} \|_2 \le 1.21 \times 10^{-13}, \qquad
    \| \mathcal{M} \|_2 \le 1.21 \times 10^{-13}.
\end{equation}

A sovereign homeostasis controller adjusts gauge parameters, refinement
criteria, and damping coefficients in response to constraint drift,
ensuring long-term stability across semiclassical, near-horizon, and
weak-field regimes.

\subsection{Semiclassical Backreaction and Source Terms}
Hawking radiation is modeled using localized negative-energy sinks
distributed near the apparent horizon. The energy density update follows:
\begin{equation}
    \rho_{\text{new}} =
    \rho_{\text{old}} +
    \frac{\dot{M} c^2}{\Delta t \, \Delta V},
\end{equation}
where $\dot{M}$ is the horizon mass-loss rate computed from the outgoing
flux. These sinks are incorporated directly into the stress-energy tensor
$T_{\mu\nu}$ and evolved consistently with the BSSN equations.

\subsection{Secure Multi-Messenger Telemetry Ingestion}
External astrophysical telemetry (Gaia DR3 astrometry, LIGO O4 strain
data) is ingested through a lattice-based post-quantum cryptographic
encapsulation layer. The encrypted data are decoded into boundary
conditions or source terms without exposing raw telemetry to the
simulation core. This ensures both numerical consistency and
cryptographic integrity.

\subsection{Parallelization and Cluster Deployment}
The architecture supports multi-node execution via MPI domain
decomposition. Each AMR block is assigned to a GPU, and inter-node
communication is performed using non-blocking MPI primitives. The system
is compatible with Kubernetes orchestration for elastic scaling across
cloud environments.

\section{Summary}
The AAAHSC--9.4 numerical framework integrates secure telemetry ingestion,
GPU-resident AMR refinement, BSSN evolution, ADM constraint monitoring,
and semiclassical backreaction into a unified architecture capable of
stable, high-fidelity relativistic simulation across multiple physical
regimes.